WEBVTT

00:00:00.673 --> 00:00:02.198
Punctuation traps.

00:00:02.198 --> 00:00:03.748
When not to punctuate.

00:00:03.748 --> 00:00:04.788
No rule?

00:00:04.788 --> 00:00:06.057
No punctuation.

00:00:07.611 --> 00:00:09.386
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:09.386 --> 00:00:12.111
One choice has no punctuation at all.

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:14.943
And there's often a long subject before the verb.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.971
It's testing whether you can resist punctuation that isn't needed.

00:00:21.011 --> 00:00:22.336
Here's the method.

00:00:22.336 --> 00:00:27.061
Name the parts on each side of the blank: subject, verb, object.

00:00:27.061 --> 00:00:31.261
If they belong together, like a subject and its verb, use no mark.

00:00:31.261 --> 00:00:34.096
Then ask: what rule allows this mark?

00:00:34.096 --> 00:00:35.985
If you can't name one, cut it.

00:00:37.073 --> 00:00:40.498
Rule one: nothing goes between a subject and its verb.

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:42.623
Not even after a long subject.

00:00:42.623 --> 00:00:43.535
No comma.

00:00:44.606 --> 00:00:48.621
Rule two: nothing goes before that, after a verb like found.

00:00:48.621 --> 00:00:50.816
Found, comma, that is wrong.

00:00:50.816 --> 00:00:51.909
Just found that.

00:00:52.973 --> 00:00:56.808
Rule three: nothing goes after such as, or including.

00:00:56.808 --> 00:00:58.248
Not such as, colon.

00:00:58.248 --> 00:01:00.209
Just such as oak and maple.

00:01:01.226 --> 00:01:02.476
Let's try one.

00:01:02.476 --> 00:01:07.716
This whole chunk, from the researchers to the Canadian Arctic, is one long subject.

00:01:07.716 --> 00:01:11.321
Then comes the verb, concluded, and then what they concluded.

00:01:11.321 --> 00:01:13.271
Those parts belong together.

00:01:13.271 --> 00:01:15.471
A comma before that splits them.

00:01:15.471 --> 00:01:18.946
A comma before concluded splits the subject from its verb.

00:01:18.946 --> 00:01:21.821
And a colon right after a verb is never right.

00:01:21.821 --> 00:01:23.521
No punctuation at all.

00:01:23.521 --> 00:01:24.808
The answer is A.

00:01:25.848 --> 00:01:27.698
Watch out for the breath comma.

00:01:27.698 --> 00:01:30.233
A long subject makes you want to pause.

00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:31.008
Don't.

00:01:31.008 --> 00:01:33.433
A pause when you read aloud isn't a rule.

00:01:34.477 --> 00:01:36.357
Here's the lesson on one page.

00:01:36.357 --> 00:01:37.422
How to spot it.

00:01:37.422 --> 00:01:38.747
The three steps.

00:01:38.747 --> 00:01:39.852
The rules.

00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:40.902
And the trap.

00:01:40.902 --> 00:01:43.667
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
